<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="3f49209e-6e61-4623-bd92-47caa0808f2e" revisionNumber="1">
	<developerConceptualDocument
		xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
		xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

		<introduction>
			<para>This section specifies the programming platforms that the libraries are targeting, and the packages that contain the libraries.</para>
		</introduction>

		<section address="Platforms">
			<title>Target Platforms</title>
			<content>
				<para>The project is currently built for five target platforms:</para>
				<list class="bullet">
					<listItem>
						<para>.NET 6 (net6.0)</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>.NET 8 (net8.0)</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>.NET 9 (net9.0)</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>.NET Framework 4.7.2 and higher (net472) for classic desktop applications and services.</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>.NET Standard 2.0 (netstandard2.0) for any other platform not covered by the previous.</para>					
					</listItem>
				</list>
				<para>
					While the libraries are almost identical in functionality for every platform, there are some differences, to provide the best possible support for every platform,
					and also adhere to best practices for every platform.
					For example, while the versions for net472 and netstandard2.0 support serialization via SerializableAttribute and ISerializable, the other platforms do not,
					at is is being considered deprecated. On the other hand, versions for net60, net80 and net90 have methods to read configurations from Spans.
					All versions support ThrowIf* methods on various Exception classes that were incrementally introduced beginning with net50.
				</para>
			</content>
		</section>

		<section address="Packages">
			<title>Packages</title>
			<content>
				<para>
					The project creates several NuGet packages that can be found on <externalLink>
						<linkText>nuget.org</linkText>
						<linkUri>https://www.nuget.org</linkUri>
					</externalLink>
					For debugging purposes, the packages support the SourceLink standard to access the sources on GitHub, but symbol packages (.snupkg) are also available on nuget.org.
				</para>
				<para>You can also download the binaries from the <externalLink>
					<linkText>Releases</linkText>
					<linkUri>https://github.com/NerdyDuck/NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions/releases</linkUri>
				</externalLink> page.</para>
				<list class="bullet">
					<listItem>
						<para>
							<externalLink>
								<linkText>NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions</linkText>
								<linkUri>https://www.nuget.org/packages/NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions</linkUri>
							</externalLink> is the central package and library containing the exceptions and accompanying helper classes, and configuration options (for facility identifier overrides and the debug mode) via XML files.</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>
							<externalLink>
								<linkText>NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.AppConfig</linkText>
								<linkUri>https://www.nuget.org/packages/NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.AppConfig</linkUri>
							</externalLink> expands the configuration options to classic app.config/web.config files.</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>
							<externalLink>
								<linkText>NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.ConfigSection</linkText>
								<linkUri>https://www.nuget.org/packages/NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.ConfigSection</linkUri>
							</externalLink> expands the configuration options to support IConfiguration (Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration).</para>
					</listItem>
					<listItem>
						<para>
							<externalLink>
								<linkText>NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.Json</linkText>
								<linkUri>https://www.nuget.org/packages/NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.Json</linkUri>
							</externalLink>NerdyDuck.CodedExceptions.Configuration.Json expands the configuration options to simple JSON files.</para>
					</listItem>
				</list>
				<para>
					All packages contain the assemblies for all platforms, as well as IntelliSense XML files.
				</para>
			</content>
		</section>

		<section address="Languages">
			<title>Languages - Localization</title>
			<content>
				<para>
					While texts in the Base Class Libraries (BCL) - mainly exception messages - are localized in .NET Framework, .NET Core and later only supplies English text resources.
					The reasoning to remove the other languages seems to be that Microsoft does not expect exception messages to be visible for the end user.
				</para>
				<para>
					I do not expect this to always be the case, and I do not expect every service professional in the world to be proficient in English.
					That is why I provide localizations of all texts in the libraries. Unfortunately, at the time being, only in English (en-us) and German (de), both included in the NuGet package.
				</para>
				<para>
					If you feel that it needs more languages, and are able to help, please contact me, or just clone the repository, add the translation, and send a pull request.
				</para>
				<para>
					If you only need the English version (included in the base libraries), just remove the other resource libraries (*.resources.dll) from your build output.
				</para>
			</content>
		</section>

		<relatedTopics></relatedTopics>

	</developerConceptualDocument>
</topic>
